xplicit control conditions guarding this read operation.\n- **Variable Value Tracking**:\n  - This read operation is part of the main task and will always execute as part of the normal flow of the program.\n- **Execution Path Analysis**:\n  - The assignment `t = g;` is unconditional and will always execute after the `if (r)` block (whether or not the block is entered).\n- **Conclusion**: The read operation `t = g;` at line 24 **can be executed**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: **Pay Attention to Control Condition Changes**\n- In this code, there are no explicit changes to control conditions caused by interrupts or other tasks. The ISR `svp_simple_699_001_isr_1` modifies the global variable `g`, but it does not alter any control conditions in the main task.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: **Check Interrupt Status**\n- The code does not explicitly disable or enable interrupts using functions like `disable_isr()` or `enable_isr()`. Therefore, we assume that interrupts are enabled by default unless otherwise stated.\n- Since the ISR `svp_simple_699_001_isr_1` can preempt the main task at any point, its write operation (`g = 42;`) can occur concurrently with the main task's operations on `g`.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Generate the Final Report\n\n#### Summary of Analysis:\n1. **Write Operation at Line 20 (`g = 17;`)**:\n   - Can be executed if `r != 0`.\n2. **Write Operation at Line 13 (`g = 42;`)**:\n   - Can be executed when the ISR is triggered.\n3. **Read Operation at Line 24 (`t = g;`)**:\n   - Always executed as part of the main task.\n\nSince all three operations can be executed under certain conditions, the atomicity violation reported in the initial defect report is valid. Concurrent access to the shared variable `g` by the main task and the ISR can lead to a race condition.\n\n---\n\n### Final Output:\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [20, 13, 